     1	# Design specification: comparison of collections by location
     2	
     3	Date: 2026-11-17
     4	Branch: `app-rewrite`
     5	Extends: `docs/specs/2026-11-17_app_rewrite_spec.md` (revision 2 with build
     6	notes a to k), as built at commit 8d387bf. Section numbers written as "main
     7	6.3" refer to that document.
     8	Status: design, revision 1, not yet audited and not yet built.
     9	
    10	## 1. Purpose
    11	
    12	The author asked for an optional feature that compares collections of bifaces
    13	from different locations, and that shows the results on a map when the
    14	coordinates of the locations are supplied. The feature must not be required:
    15	the app must work as it does now for a user who has one group of bifaces and no
    16	locations file.
    17	
    18	The feature answers three descriptive questions for a student:
    19	
    20	1. How different are the mean outline shapes of the collections?
    21	2. How much of the shape variation lies between collections, and is that more
    22	   than random grouping of the same specimens produces?
    23	3. Do collections that are closer together on the ground have more similar
    24	   mean shapes?
    25	
    26	## 2. Decisions made by the author (2026-11-17)
    27	
    28	- Scale of use: a class exercise, about 3 to 6 locations with 5 to 20 bifaces
    29	  each. Thresholds are set for that case.
    30	- Map: a static map with coastline and boundary outlines from the `maps`
    31	  package, identical in the app and the PDF report. No interactive map and no
    32	  requests to outside servers.
    33	- Inference: descriptive in the app. The app reports effect sizes with
    34	  random-reassignment references and exports scores by collection. No model is
    35	  fitted in the app. This uses exception (c) of the shared conventions
    36	  (descriptive methods). A multilevel model fitted in Stan to the exported
    37	  scores is the route to any published inference and is outside this
    38	  specification.
    39	- The live app is not changed by this work. The feature is built on the branch
    40	  and deployed to the test address only.
    41	
    42	## 3. Scope
    43	
    44	In scope: a "Collections" sub-tab in step 4, a locations file, six figures,
    45	interpretation text, two CSV downloads, report pages, tests, and checklist
    46	items.
    47	
    48	Out of scope: any change to image processing, outlines, alignment, Fourier
    49	analysis, or the existing tabs. Ordinations that maximize separation between
    50	groups (canonical variates, between-group PCA) are excluded, because with more
    51	variables than specimens they show separation between groups that are random
    52	samples of one population [CITE-CHECK: source for this property of
    53	between-group PCA; no PDF is in the repository]. A demonstration dataset is out
    54	of scope because the repository holds four sample images and no set of
    55	provenanced collections (section 12).
    56	
    57	## 4. Definitions and inputs
    58	
    59	### 4.1 Collections
    60	
    61	A collection is the set of saved specimens that share a group label (main 6.5),
    62	compared exactly after removing leading and trailing spaces. Specimens with an
    63	empty label belong to no collection.
    64	
    65	An eligible collection has at least 3 specimens. The comparison needs at least
    66	2 eligible collections. Specimens that are unlabelled or in a collection with
    67	fewer than 3 specimens are left out of every collection statistic, and the tab
    68	states how many were left out and names the collections concerned.
    69	
    70	All specimens, including those left out here, remain in the alignment, the
    71	Fourier analysis, and the PCA of the other tabs. The collection statistics
    72	therefore use the same aligned shapes, the same number of harmonics H, the same
    73	symmetric-shape option, and the same PC axes as the rest of step 4. A
    74	consequence, stated in the tab, is that adding or removing an unlabelled
    75	specimen can change the collection results slightly through the alignment.
    76	
    77	Shape data. X is the N by 4H matrix of Fourier coefficients (main 7.2) of the N
    78	specimens in eligible collections. Row i is x_i. Collection g has n_g specimens
    79	and mean vector m_g. The grand mean of the N rows is c.
    80	
    81	### 4.2 Locations file (optional)
    82	
    83	A CSV file with a header row and the columns `collection`, `latitude`,
    84	`longitude`. Column names are matched without regard to case or surrounding
    85	spaces. Other columns are ignored. Latitude and longitude are decimal degrees
    86	on WGS84, north and east positive.
    87	
    88	The file is rejected with a message, and any previously loaded locations are
    89	kept, when: a required column is missing; the file has no rows or more than 200
    90	rows; a collection name is empty or repeated; a coordinate is missing, not a
    91	number, or not finite; a latitude is outside [-90, 90]; or a longitude is
    92	outside [-180, 180]. The file size limit of the outline file (main 6.5) applies.
    93	
    94	Rows are matched to collections by exact name after trimming. The tab lists
    95	rows that match no collection and eligible collections that have no row. A
    96	located collection is an eligible collection with a row. Two collections may
    97	have the same coordinates.
    98	
    99	Locations are held for the session only and are not written to the outline
   100	file. A "Clear locations" button removes them. A "Download a locations
   101	template" button returns a CSV with the three columns and one row per current
   102	collection name, with empty coordinates.
   103	
   104	The tab states that the file is sent to the server for the session, and that
   105	coordinates of protected sites should be rounded or replaced with an
   106	approximate position before upload.
   107	
   108	## 5. Statistics
   109	
   110	All statistics are computed in `R/collections.R` from X, the collection labels,
   111	and the locations. Random reassignments use 999 draws and seed 1, and restore
   112	the caller's random number state, as `region_rv` does (main 7.6).
   113	
   114	### 5.1 Mean shapes
   115	
   116	For each eligible collection the mean coefficient vector m_g is passed through
   117	the inverse transform (main 7.2) with x0 = y0 = 0. The mean shapes are drawn
   118	overlaid, one colour per collection.
   119	
   120	### 5.2 Share of shape variation between collections
   121	
   122	    SST = sum_i |x_i - c|^2
   123	    SSW = sum_g sum_{i in g} |x_i - m_g|^2
   124	    SSB = sum_g n_g |m_g - c|^2 = SST - SSW
   125	
   126	The reported value is SSB / SST. It is not available when X has no variation
   127	(main build note i).
   128	
   129	Reference: the N specimens are reassigned to collections at random, keeping the
   130	collection sizes, and SSB / SST is recomputed. The mean and the 2.5 to 97.5
   131	percent range of the reassigned values are shown beside the observed value.
   132	With random grouping the expected share is (G - 1) / (N - 1) for G collections,
   133	which is far from zero in small samples: 2 / 14 = 0.14 for 3 collections of 5. The tab
   134	states this number for the current data. No p-value is reported.
   135	
   136	### 5.3 Distance between collection means
   137	
   138	For collections g and h:
   139	
   140	    raw:        d_gh  = |m_g - m_h|
   141	    v_g         = sum_{i in g} |x_i - m_g|^2 / (n_g - 1)
   142	    corrected:  dc_gh = sqrt(max(0, d_gh^2 - v_g / n_g - v_h / n_h))
   143	
   144	The raw distance is larger on average than the distance between the population
   145	means, because each sample mean carries sampling error of expected squared size
   146	v_g / n_g. The quantity under the square root is an unbiased estimate of the
   147	squared distance between population means when the specimens of each collection
   148	are independent draws from that collection's population. dc_gh is 0 when the
   149	raw distance is no larger than sampling error of that size produces. The square
   150	root of an unbiased estimate is not itself unbiased, and the tab calls dc_gh an
   151	approximate correction.
   152	
   153	Reference for each pair: the n_g + n_h specimens of the two collections are
   154	reassigned between them at random, keeping the two sizes, and the raw distance
   155	is recomputed. The mean and the 2.5 to 97.5 percent range are shown beside the
   156	observed raw distance. The tab states the number of pairs and that, with a 95
   157	percent range and several pairs, an observed value can fall outside its range
   158	through sampling alone.
   159	
   160	Outputs: a pair table (collections, sizes, raw distance, reference mean and
   161	range, corrected distance), a figure of observed raw distances against their
   162	reference ranges in the form of the region RV figure, and a matrix figure of
   163	corrected distances. Distances are in units of the coefficients of
   164	unit-centroid-size shapes and are comparable only within one analysis.
   165	
   166	No tree of collections is drawn. With 3 to 6 collections a tree adds nothing to
   167	the matrix and suggests a branching order that the data cannot support.
   168	
   169	### 5.4 Variation within each collection
   170	
   171	v_g of section 5.3 is the within-collection shape variance. Its expected value
   172	does not depend on n_g, so collections of different sizes can be compared. Its
   173	precision does depend on n_g. To show this, v_g is recomputed with each
   174	specimen of the collection left out in turn, and the smallest and largest of
   175	those values are drawn as a range around v_g. The range describes sensitivity
   176	to single specimens. It is not an uncertainty interval, and the tab says so.
   177	
   178	### 5.5 Collection summaries
   179	
   180	For each eligible collection: n_g; the mean score on each PC of the existing
   181	PCA (main 7.3) for up to the first 3 components; the mean of length divided by
   182	width; the mean position of maximum width (main 6.4); v_g; and the mean length
   183	in mm. Mean length is given only when every specimen in every eligible
   184	collection has a scale.
   185	
   186	### 5.6 Shape distance against geographic distance
   187	
   188	Requires at least 5 located collections with at least two distinct positions.
   189	With fewer, the tab states the requirement and the number available. Three
   190	locations give 3 pairs and four give 6, which cannot show a relationship.
   191	
   192	Geographic distance is the great-circle distance by the haversine formula on a
   193	sphere of radius 6371.0088 km:
   194	
   195	    h = sin^2((lat2 - lat1) / 2) + cos(lat1) cos(lat2) sin^2((lon2 - lon1) / 2)
   196	    distance = 2 R asin(min(1, sqrt(h)))
   197	
   198	The figure plots dc_gh against distance in km, one labelled point per pair. The
   199	reported value r is the Pearson correlation between the two sets of pairwise
   200	distances, the matrix correlation of the Mantel test [CITE-CHECK: Mantel 1967;
   201	no PDF is in the repository]. It is not available when either set has no
   202	variation.
   203	
   204	Reference: the positions are reassigned among the located collections and r is
   205	recomputed. All G! assignments are used when G is at most 7 (5040 assignments),
   206	and 999 random assignments otherwise. The mean and the 2.5 to 97.5 percent
   207	range are reported with r. The tab states the number of pairs and the number of
   208	assignments used, and that with 5 locations the reference has at most 120
   209	values. No p-value is reported.
   210	
   211	## 6. Map
   212	
   213	Shown when at least 2 collections are located. Collections that are eligible
   214	and not located are named below the map.
   215	
   216	The map is a ggplot2 figure. Each located collection is a point at its
   217	longitude and latitude, labelled with its name above the point, and coloured on
   218	the viridis scale by a quantity the student selects from section 5.5: mean PC1
   219	score (default), mean PC2 score, mean length divided by width, mean position of
   220	maximum width, within-collection variance, number of specimens, and mean length
   221	in mm when available.
   222	
   223	Window. The bounding box of the located collections is extended by 20 percent
   224	of its span on each side, with a minimum span of 0.5 degrees of latitude and
   225	the equivalent ground distance in longitude at the middle latitude. The aspect
   226	ratio is set by `coord_quickmap`.
   227	
   228	Background. Country polygons from the `world` database of the `maps` package
   229	are drawn as a light fill with thin grey boundaries. State boundaries from the
   230	`state` database are added when the window overlaps the contiguous United
   231	States (longitude -125 to -66, latitude 24 to 50), and county boundaries from
   232	the `county` database when, in addition, the window spans less than 3 degrees
   233	of latitude. A scale bar in km is drawn, with a length of 1, 2, or 5 times a
   234	power of ten nearest to one quarter of the window's width at the middle
   235	latitude.
   236	
   237	Limits, stated below the map. The outlines are low resolution and are for
   238	orientation only. Outside the contiguous United States a window smaller than a
   239	country shows no boundary. Collections on both sides of the 180 degree meridian
   240	are not drawn correctly, although their distances in section 5.6 are correct.
   241	Labels of collections that are close together can overlap.
   242	
   243	The author chose the static map knowing it has no terrain, rivers, or roads.
   244	Section 12 records the consequence for small study areas.
   245	
   246	## 7. Interface
   247	
   248	A sub-tab "Collections" is added to step 4 after "Regions". It is always
   249	present. With fewer than 2 eligible collections it shows only a short
   250	explanation: group labels entered in step 2 or step 3 define collections, each
   251	needs at least 3 specimens, and a locations file is optional. The other tabs
   252	and the sidebar are unchanged, and no existing control gains a meaning it did
   253	not have.
   254	
   255	With 2 or more eligible collections the tab shows, in this order:
   256	
   257	1. A sentence giving the collections in the analysis with their sizes, and any
   258	   specimens left out (section 4.1).
   259	2. Mean shapes (5.1) beside the "How to read this" text for the tab.
   260	3. Share between collections with its reference (5.2).
   261	4. Pair distances with references, the matrix of corrected distances, and the
   262	   pair table (5.3).
   263	5. Within-collection variance with leave-one-out ranges (5.4).
   264	6. A "Locations" card: the file input, the template download, the clear button,
   265	   the privacy statement (4.2), the list of unmatched names, the selector for
   266	   the mapped quantity, the map (6), and the distance figure (5.6) or the
   267	   sentence saying why it is withheld.
   268	
   269	The PCA tab's "Colour specimens by: group label" option already colours the
   270	morphospace by collection. The tab's text points to it.
   271	
   272	Downloads added to the sidebar: "Collection summary (CSV)" with the columns of
   273	section 5.5, and "Collection distances (CSV)" with the pair table of 5.3 and
   274	the distance in km where both collections are located. Neither file contains
   275	coordinates. Both are empty files with a header when the comparison is not
   276	available.
   277	
   278	Report. When the comparison is available, the PDF gains one page for each of
   279	the figures above with its text. The map page is included only when the box
   280	"Include the map in the PDF report" in the Locations card is ticked (default
   281	ticked). The card states that the map shows the positions of the collections.
   282	
   283	Categorical colours use the Okabe-Ito palette for up to 8 collections. With
   284	more than 8 the discrete viridis scale is used, because the present
   285	`category_scale` repeats colours beyond 8 levels.
   286	
   287	## 8. Interpretation text
   288	
   289	Built in `R/interpret.R` from computed values, under the rules of main
   290	section 9. Content:
   291	
   292	- The collections compared, their sizes, and the specimens left out.
   293	- The observed share between collections, the share expected from random
   294	  grouping at these sizes, and the reference range.
   295	- The pair with the largest and the pair with the smallest corrected distance,
   296	  and the pairs whose corrected distance is 0.
   297	- The collections with the largest and smallest within-collection variance and
   298	  their ratio, with the caution about small collections.
   299	- For the distance figure: r, its reference range, the number of pairs, and the
   300	  statement that a few locations cannot establish a spatial pattern.
   301	
   302	Standing cautions, shown in full on the tab and in the report:
   303	
   304	- A difference between collections is a difference between these samples. It
   305	  can arise from the age of the artifacts, raw material, resharpening and
   306	  breakage, how each collection was gathered, or local practice. The analysis
   307	  does not distinguish among these.
   308	- Left and right in an outline depend on which face was photographed. Unless
   309	  every collection was photographed face-consistently, tick "Analyse symmetric
   310	  shapes" before comparing collections.
   311	- Collections photographed or outlined by different people, at different image
   312	  resolutions, or with different detection settings can differ for that reason
   313	  alone. Edge roughness from image resolution affects the higher harmonics.
   314	- The PC axes describe variation among all specimens. They are not the
   315	  directions that best separate the collections.
   316	
   317	## 9. Dependencies and layout
   318	
   319	One runtime package is added: `maps`. It is not installed on the development
   320	machine and must be installed before the build. It is used through
   321	`ggplot2::map_data`. New file `bifaceAnalysis/R/collections.R` holds the
   322	statistics, the locations file reader, and the distance function. Figures go in
   323	`R/plots.R` and text in `R/interpret.R`. New test file
   324	`tests/testthat/test-collections.R`.
   325	
   326	## 10. Testing and acceptance
   327	
   328	Unit tests, tolerances absolute unless stated.
   329	
   330	Partition
   331	- For simulated coefficient matrices with 3 collections of unequal size,
   332	  SSB + SSW equals SST to 1e-10 relative, and SSB / SST equals the value from
   333	  `summary(lm(X ~ factor))` sums of squares pooled over columns.
   334	- Collections that are identical copies of one set of rows give a share of 0.
   335	  Collections with no within-collection variation and different means give 1.
   336	- The mean of the reassigned shares over 999 draws is within 0.02 of
   337	  (G - 1) / (N - 1) for a simulated matrix with no collection effect.
   338	- The reference is identical on repeated calls, and the caller's random number
   339	  state is unchanged by the call.
   340	
   341	Distances
   342	- Raw distance equals the Euclidean distance between row means.
   343	- Corrected distance: over 4000 simulated pairs of samples (sizes 5 and 8, 12
   344	  variables, known mean difference of length 2 and of length 0), the mean of
   345	  the quantity under the square root is within 3 Monte Carlo standard errors of
   346	  4 and of 0. The mean of the raw squared distance exceeds 4 by more than 3
   347	  standard errors in the first case.
   348	- A negative value under the root is reported as 0.
   349	- Leave-one-out ranges contain v_g for a collection of identical rows (all
   350	  zero) and bracket the values computed by hand for a 4-specimen example.
   351	
   352	Geography
   353	- Haversine: (0, 0) to (0, 1) is 111.195 km within 0.001. The equator to the
   354	  pole is 10007.543 km within 0.001. A point to itself is 0. Two points across
   355	  the 180 degree meridian at the equator, longitudes 179.5 and -179.5, are
   356	  111.195 km apart.
   357	- Matrix correlation: equals `cor` of the lower triangles. With 5 located
   358	  collections all 120 assignments are used and include the identity. When shape
   359	  distance is set equal to geographic distance, r is 1 and is the largest
   360	  reference value.
   361	- Not available (no error) when all positions coincide.
   362	
   363	Locations file
   364	- A valid file with mixed-case headers, extra columns, and surrounding spaces
   365	  is read. Each rejection condition of section 4.2 returns its message and
   366	  leaves earlier locations in place. Unmatched names on each side are listed.
   367	
   368	Thresholds
   369	- 1 eligible collection, 2 eligible collections, a collection of 2 specimens,
   370	  unlabelled specimens, 4 located collections, and 5 located collections each
   371	  give the outputs and messages of sections 4 to 6, with no error.
   372	
   373	Figures and report
   374	- Each new plotting function returns a ggplot object that builds without error
   375	  or warning for 2, 3, 6, and 9 collections, and for a window inside and
   376	  outside the contiguous United States. The map test is skipped with a message
   377	  when `maps` is not installed, and the full suite is run with it installed
   378	  before acceptance.
   379	- The report has the expected number of pages with and without locations and
   380	  with the map box unticked.
   381	
   382	Server tests (`shiny::testServer`)
   383	- The existing outputs are unchanged when group labels are added: values of the
   384	  PCA, cluster, and region reactives are identical before and after.
   385	- Loading, replacing, and clearing a locations file; the two downloads have the
   386	  stated columns and no coordinate columns.
   387	
   388	Existing tests continue to pass unchanged.
   389	
   390	Manual checklist items are added for: the explanation with no collections; the
   391	tab with 3 labelled collections built from the sample images by repeated
   392	upload; loading a locations file and a rejected file; the map inside and
   393	outside the United States; the selector for the mapped quantity; the template
   394	download; the map box in the report; and the narrow layout of the tab.
   395	
   396	Acceptance: all automated tests pass, the checklist items are run on the test
   397	deployment, and the Codex build audit has no blocking finding open.
   398	
   399	## 11. Performance
   400	
   401	With 6 collections of 20 specimens and H = 30, X is 120 by 120. The partition
   402	reference is 999 computations of group means. The pair references are 15 pairs
   403	of 999 each. The matrix correlation uses at most 5040 assignments of a 7 by 7
   404	matrix. Each is expected to finish in well under one second, and the build
   405	must confirm a total under 2 seconds on the development machine for this case.
   406	The statistics are computed in one reactive that depends on the coefficient
   407	matrix, the labels, and the locations, so that changing the mapped quantity
   408	does not repeat them.
   409	
   410	## 12. Open points
   411	
   412	1. No example data. The four sample images cannot form collections. The tests
   413	   use simulated coefficient matrices, and the checklist uses the sample images
   414	   uploaded repeatedly under different labels, which exercises the interface
   415	   and not the archaeology. A demonstration needs provenanced images from the
   416	   author.
   417	2. Map context for small areas. A class project with sites a few kilometres
   418	   apart gets county lines in the United States and an empty background
   419	   elsewhere. The scale bar and labels remain. An interactive map with tiles
   420	   would give context and was not chosen.
   421	3. Two citations are flagged and unverified: Mantel 1967, and a source for the
